{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Colorado Rockies were a team defined by their ceiling — thin air, a few bright moments, and a pitching staff that too often turned close games into routs. Opening the season 1-3 in March, they stumbled into April with a rotation and bullpen that bled runs at an alarming rate. Jalen Beeks, tasked with holding leads time and again, became the emblem of a relief corps that couldn't strand runners. Yet even in those dark early days, Ryan McMahon announced himself with a grand slam that briefly made Coors Field electric, a reminder that this lineup had teeth even when the season felt like it was slipping away before May arrived.\n\nMay offered the Rockies their most sustained stretch of competitiveness, finishing 14-13 — the only winning month on the calendar. The bullpen remained a sieve, but clutch moments emerged: McMahon's ninth home run, a pair of game-saving double plays, and Jacob Stallings going deep to rescue an afternoon in the thin mountain air. It was the closest Colorado came to feeling like a legitimate contender, even as the losses piled up around the edges and the ERA ballooned toward historic territory. For a few weeks, the pieces seemed to fit.\n\nJune and the first half of July brought the collapse that had been lurking all along. A 7-20 June dismantled any optimism May had built. Victor Vodnik served up a three-run shot to Teoscar Hernández with Shohei Ohtani on base — the kind of gut-punch that captures a season in one at-bat. The Rockies were overmatched, outpitched, and outgunned, their 5.47 ERA a testament to a staff without answers. July steadied things slightly at 11-15, buoyed by a remarkable single game on July 22nd when Sam Hilliard, Jake Cave, and Ezequiel Tovar combined for a rally that felt like a fever dream against Boston.\n\nThe final months were a slow fade into autumn. Brendan Rodgers had a big double in August, Sam Hilliard homered again in September, and Michael Toglia's three-run blast offered a flash of what youth might bring in future years. But the closing image of 2024 belonged to Shohei Ohtani, who crushed his 53rd home run off Seth Halvorsen on September 22nd — a fitting coda to a 61-101 season in which Colorado too often played the role of stepping stone for the league's elite. The future would have to wait.",
